WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.640
We now have a trapped
door for solving phi.

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:06.660
If you know the factorization
for N, then finding phi N

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:07.700
is easy.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.880
For example, the prime
factorization of 77

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.470
is 7 times 11.

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:17.460
So phi of 77 is 6 times 10-- 60.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.460
Step three, how to
connect the phi function

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.790
to modular exponentiation.

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:25.930
For this, he turned
to Euler's Theorem,

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:27.940
which is a relationship
between the phi

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:31.770
function and modular
exponentiation as follows.

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:38.320
m to the power of phi n
is congruent to 1 mod n.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.010
This means you could pick
any two numbers, such

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.030
that they do not
share a common factor.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:45.180
Let's call them m then n.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.180
Say m equals 5 and n equals 8.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:53.630
Now, when you raise m to
the power of phi n, or 4,

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:58.090
and divide by n, you will
always be left with 1.

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:00.900
Now we just need to
modify this equation using

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.310
two simple rules.

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:06.210
First, if you raise the
number 1 to any exponent k,

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.230
you always get 1.

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:11.160
In the same way, we can
multiply the exponents phi n

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.420
by any number k, and
the solution is still 1.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:19.720
Second, if you multiply
1 by any number, say m,

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:21.200
it always equals m.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.470
In the same way, we can
multiply the left side by m

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:27.160
to get m on the right-hand side.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:31.410
And this can be simplified as
m to the power of k times phi

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.600
n plus 1.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.270
This is the breakthrough.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:37.060
We now have an
equation for finding

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:41.900
the e times d, which
depends on phi n.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:44.520
Therefore, it's
easy to calculate

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:50.310
d only if the factorization
of n is known,

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:53.200
meaning d should be
Alice's private key.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.980
It's the trap door
which will allow

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.800
her to undo the effect of e.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:01.790
Let's do a simple example to
see all of this in action.

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:03.780
Say Bob has a
message he converted

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.610
into a number using
a padding scheme.

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:08.590
Let's call this m.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:13.200
Then Alice generates her public
and private key as follows.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.790
First, she generates two random
prime numbers of similar size

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:21.590
and multiplies them
to get n-- 3,127.

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:23.900
Then she calculates
phi of n easily,

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:25.620
since she knows
the factorization

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:30.440
of n, which turns out to 3,016.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:34.200
Next, she picks some
small public exponent e

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:37.200
with the condition that it must
be an odd number that does not

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.390
share a factor with phi n.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:42.620
This case, she picks e equals 3.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:46.530
Finally, she finds the value
of her private exponent

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:53.180
d, which in this case is 2 times
phi of n, plus 1, divided by 3,

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:55.990
or 2,011.

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:59.290
Now, she hides everything
except the value of n

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:03.760
and e, because n and e
make up her public key.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:05.930
Think of it as an open lock.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:10.150
She sends this to Bob to
lock his message with.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:12.690
Bob locks his message
by calculating m

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:16.370
to the power of e mod n.

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:19.170
Call this c, his
encrypted message,

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.030
which he sends it back to Alice.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:23.190
Finally, Alice
decrypts his message

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:28.010
using her private key, d,
accessed through her trap door.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:33.900
c to the power of d mod n
equals Bob's original message m.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.110
Notice that Eve, or
anyone else with c, n,

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:42.580
and e can only find the
exponent d if they can calculate

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:45.310
phi n, which requires
that they know

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:47.920
the prime factorization of n.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:50.940
If n is large enough,
Alice can be sure

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.590
that this will take
hundreds of years

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:56.010
even with the most powerful
network of computers.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:57.880
This trick was
immediately classified

00:03:57.880 --> 00:03:59.230
after its publication.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:03.170
However, it was independently
rediscovered in 1977

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:06.410
by Ron Rivest, Adi Shamir,
and Len Adleman, which

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:10.010
is why it's now known
as RSA encryption.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:13.310
RSA is the most widely
used public key algorithm

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:17.350
in the world and the most
copied software in history.

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:19.220
Every internet user
on Earth is using

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:23.450
RSA or some variant of it,
whether they realize it or not.

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:25.420
Its strength relies
on the hardness

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:29.450
of prime factorization, which
is a result of deep questions

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:32.300
about the distribution
of prime numbers

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:35.040
a question which has
remained unsolved

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.370
for thousands of years.

